l avoid triple quotes in Python code if possible, or escape them. Better to use single quotes in Python for strings, then the JSON string uses double quotes. But Python code may have double quotes inside (e.g., inside dict literals). We'll use single quotes for any string literals in Python if possible, but not always. We'll just escape any double quotes in the Python source with backslash. Also escape backslashes. Also newlines become \\n.\n\nSimplify: Write Python code as a single line? But that's ugly. We'll write multi-line with escaped newlines. It's acceptable.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON manually.\n\nLet's draft Python solution:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    word_tokens = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\n    forced = set(case['forced_words'])\n    budget = case['token_budget']\n    n = len(word_tokens)\n    forced_mask = 0\n    for i in forced:\n        forced_mask |= (1 << i)\n    best_mask = 0\n    best_score = -1.0\n    # Precompute token sets for each word to avoid repeated set creation\n    token_sets = [set(tokens) for tokens in word_tokens]\n    # Check forced words disjointness? Not needed if union size check covers.\n    for mask in range(1 << n):\n        if (mask & forced_mask) != forced_mask:\n            continue\n        # Compute union\n        union = set()\n        total_score = 0.0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if mask & (1 << i):\n                union.update(token_sets[i])\n                total_score += word_scores[i]\n                # Early break if union exceeds budget? But not necessary.\n        if len(union) <= budget:\n            if total_score > best_score:\n                best_score = total_score\n                best_mask = mask\n    # Build selected words list\n    selected = [i for i in range(n) if best_mask & (1 << i)]\n    return {\"selected_words\": selected, \"score\": best_score}\n```\n\nBut we need to handle case where no feasible solution: best_score remains -1. Should 